(project convention-rust-cargo
 (doc "rust-cargo convention: crates as build units, modules as spec components, and a line-comment carrier for actionable drift on Rust projects.")
 (status planned)
 (no-inline
 )
 (section definition
  (doc "What (convention rust-cargo) declares about project structure")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        A root .borg file declaring (convention rust-cargo) gets these
        structural rules.

        WORKSPACE LAYOUT:
        - Cargo.toml at the root is a workspace manifest; members are
          the crates.
        - crates/NAME/ or NAME/ each hold one crate with their own
          Cargo.toml. A single-crate repo has src/ at the root.
        - src/lib.rs is a library target; src/main.rs or src/bin/NAME.rs
          is a binary target.
        - tests/ holds integration tests; each file there is its own
          crate by the compiler's rules.
        - benches/, examples/ are additional cargo targets.
        - target/ is build output and is never scanned.

        UNITS AND COMPONENTS (the two levels):
        - UNIT = crate. `cargo build`/`cargo test` operate here. A crate
          that no .borg section admits to owning is drift.
        - COMPONENT = module file. A section like transcript-parse maps
          to src/transcript_parse.rs inside a crate. A module file with
          no section is drift.
        - The spec names components; the crate is context. Where a
          crate is a single module (src/lib.rs plus submodules), a
          section may target the crate itself with (implements src/lib.rs).

        NAMING:
        - Section name kebab-case -> module file snake_case:
          transcript-parse -> src/transcript_parse.rs.
        - Section name matching a crate directory -> that crate's
          lib.rs (cargo package names are kebab; Rust module names are
          snake; the mapping is mechanical both ways).
        - A (mod ...) declaration is not a component; it is internal
          structure and borge does not require a section per mod.

        DOC COMMENTS (carrier):
        - Rust has /// doc comments, // line comments, and /* */ block
          comments (which nest).
        - The borge grammar in Rust uses the LINE carrier, not the
          block carrier:
              // (* agent note (|
              //   WHAT: Parse transcript lines into events.
              //   WHY: The timeline needs structured events, not text.
              // |) *)
              pub fn parse_transcript(...)
          Rationale: /* (* ... *) */ is valid Rust but reads as
          lunacy next to ///. The line carrier keeps the grammar
          visible while the code still looks like Rust.
        - The block form stays supported (it is what Go uses, and the
          carrier is a convention field) but is not the default for
          rust-cargo and should not appear in new Rust code.
        - /// doc comments are the language's documentation; they count
          as documented for doc coverage but carry no authorship or
          literacy score. Lint rules that require (| ... |) content
          apply only to the borge form.

        VERIFY VOCABULARY:
        - rust-cargo: build, test, clippy, bench, smoke
          (build = cargo build --workspace; test = cargo test
          --workspace; clippy = cargo clippy; bench = cargo bench)

        EXCLUDED DIRECTORIES:
        - target, .git, and any directory named in .gitignore at a
          crate root.

        DRIFT LAYERS:
        - Spec drift: a section claims implemented and its component
          file is absent, or its verify apparatus is missing.
        - Code drift: a crate or module file that no section admits to
          owning.
        - Structural drift: domain logic in src/main.rs or src/bin/
          when the convention says lib targets own logic; a crate with
          no Cargo.toml; a src/ file outside any target's module tree.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section unit-map
  (doc "Section to file resolution for Rust")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        component_paths is derived, not guessed:

          1. Explicit (implements path ...) wins, always. Relative to
             the .borg file's directory.
          2. Exact component match: kebab->snake the section name and
             look for MODULE.rs in every crate's src/ tree. Found in
             exactly one crate -> that path. Found in several -> drift
             (ambiguous-component); the spec must disambiguate with
             (implements).
          3. Crate match: if the section name equals a crate's package
             name (kebab) or its directory name, the target is that
             crate's src/lib.rs, or src/main.rs for a bin-only crate.
          4. Otherwise: unresolved. A planned section with no target
             path is fine; an implemented section with no resolvable
             path is spec drift.

        Rules 2 and 3 exist because a section may describe either a
        module ("this parses transcripts") or a crate ("this is the
        timeline server"). Borge must not silently pick one.

        What is NOT a mapping:
          - Rust's module tree (mod foo; in lib.rs) is not a path
            declaration. Files can exist without a mod declaring them
            (dead files), and mods can be declared for nonexistent
            files (compile error). Drift reports the first case; the
            second is cargo's problem.
          - Macro-generated modules (include!, macro_rules) are
            invisible to file scanning. Sections covering them need
            explicit (implements) or should be specced against the
            macro's own file.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section discovery
  (doc "cargo metadata is the source of truth for crates and targets")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        `cargo metadata --format-version 1 --no-deps` returns, as JSON,
        every workspace member with its targets, target kinds
        (lib/bin/test/bench/example), and src_path. That is exactly the
        unit_spec shape from convention-abstract §unit-model, and it is
        authoritative in a way manifest parsing never is (path
        dependencies, workspace inheritance, [lib] path overrides,
        auto-discovered bins and tests).

        Fallback when cargo is unavailable or metadata fails:
          - Parse the workspace Cargo.toml [workspace] members list.
          - For each member, parse [package] name and [lib]/[[bin]]
            path overrides, defaulting to src/lib.rs and src/main.rs.
          - Auto-discovered targets (src/bin/*.rs, tests/*.rs) by
            directory convention.
        The fallback is a degraded mode, not an equal mode: borge
        reports Unit_discovery_degraded once per run so a silent
        difference from cargo's view is visible.

        Component surfaces:
          - Exported symbols: `pub` items at module top level. Parsing
            Rust's visibility grammar by hand is a trap (pub(crate),
            pub(in path), re-exports, pub use with rename). Preferred:
            `cargo doc --no-deps --document-private-items` output, or
            rustdoc JSON (--output-format json, nightly). If neither is
            available, a conservative regex over `^pub (fn|struct|enum|
            trait|const|static|mod|type|use)` is acceptable for
            coverage counting but must be labelled inferred, exactly as
            Surface.ml labels its .ml inference.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section carrier-rust
  (doc "Line-comment carrier details that are Rust-specific")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Shared adapter mechanics live in convention-abstract §carrier.
        Rust specifics:

          // (* agent note (|
          //   WHAT: ...
          // |) *)
          pub fn thing()

        - The adapter strips "//" plus ONE following space, preserving
          additional indentation. This keeps the (| ... |) body aligned
          in the source, so the printed book reads the same as the code.
        - /// doc comments are NOT carrier lines. They are the language's
          own docs, and folding them into the borge grammar would either
          lose them from rustdoc or duplicate them.
        - A run may mix // and //! *only* if //! is at the top of
          lib.rs (module docs); otherwise the run ends at the change.
        - rustfmt: the carrier is inside a comment, so rustfmt leaves it
          alone. No formatter integration needed. `borge fmt` must not
          rewrite inside carrier runs either.
        - Attributes (#[cfg(test)], #[derive(..)]) between the carrier
          run and the item do not break attachment: doc attachment
          resolves to the next non-attribute item line, which is the
          same rule Doc_detect already uses for OCaml's val/let.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section bootstrap
  (doc "Spec'ing an existing or new Rust crate without drowning")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        The onboarding problem is not the convention; it is that a
        200-module crate produces 200 unspecified-component findings on
        day one. Three mechanisms, in order of preference:

        1. (untracked path "reason") on the root project for code that
           is deliberately outside the spec (generated bindings, vendored
           deps, build scripts). This exists in drift today
           (extract_untracked, drift.ml:319) and needs no new mechanism.
        2. Coarse-first spec chapters: one section per crate that says
           what the crate is, marked implemented, explicitly stating it
           does not yet cover its modules. Components stay unspecified
           until a chapter claims them. Drift distinguishes
           unspecified-component-inside-specced-crate from
           unspecified-component-alone — the first is a todo, the second
           is a hole.
        3. (status deprecated) sections for code being retired, so the
           exit path is also spec'd.

        For a NEW project the order is the other way round and much
        cheaper: write the chapters, mark them planned, then implement.
        That is the whole point of building in these languages with
        borge rather than retrofitting.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section modules
  (doc "New modules for rust-cargo")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        New (each is one registration line plus one module — see
        convention-abstract §convention-record):
          lib/convention/convention_rust.ml   — the record above
          lib/convention/rust_metadata.ml     — cargo metadata parse + fallback
          lib/convention/rust_lexer.ml        — pub-item scan + mod tree
        Reused unchanged:
          lib/convention/carrier_line.ml      — the adapter, shared with Py/Rb
          lib/doc/doc_detect.ml               — carrier-supplied offsets only

        No new drift module. If implementing rust-cargo requires editing
        lib/check/drift.ml, Phase 0 was not finished — stop and fix the
        abstraction rather than adding a check_rust_code_drift.

        Acceptance for this chapter:
          1. `borge drift` on a rust-cargo root lists crates, targets,
             and components correctly (spot-check against
             `cargo metadata`).
          2. A carrier run above a pub fn is read as a Borg_note, and
             borge report shows its status.
          3. A module file with no section is reported once, as
             unspecified-component, with its crate as context.
          4. `borge check --worktree` runs cargo build and cargo test,
             not dune.
          5. Removing a carrier run makes lint report the missing
             annotation (with --code-doc), proving the adapter is
             actually feeding Doc_detect.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section open-questions
  (doc "Rust decisions to settle during implementation")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        1. Workspace vs single-crate .borg placement. One root book
           with (inline) per crate matches how lib.borg indexes lib/.
           Consistent answer: root book inlines one chapter per crate,
           mirroring the directory.

        2. rustdoc JSON requires nightly. Decide whether surfacing is
           nightly-optional (better) or regex-only (simpler). If regex,
           the "inferred" label must be visible in report output, not
           buried in meta.

        3. tests/ files are separate crates to cargo but conceptually
           belong to the module they test. Proposal: a tests/x.rs file
           is not required to have its own section when its name matches
           an existing section plus a test suffix (tests/transcript.rs
           for section transcript-parse). Confirm during implementation;
           if it produces false negatives, require explicit (implements).

        4. Whether clippy is a verify method or folded into build.
           Keeping it separate is better: clippy failures are quality,
           build failures are existence, and borge should be able to
           report them differently.
      |))
  )
 )
)
